  [1mThe fork:[0m there is no user ID anywhere in this app at login time. The form collects a username and a password, and
  nothing else. So "add a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter" has to mean one of three different things, and they produce different
  signatures and different amounts of work.

  - [1mClient-generated tracking ID[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m where [38;5;153muserId[39m is an ID the client mints (e.g. a UUID
    persisted in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m) to correlate this login attempt. Buys you tracking with no backend dependency; costs a
    new ID-generation concern in the client; rules nothing out. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mUsername [3mis[0;1m the identity; rename what you log[0m — no new parameter; the tracking comes from the [38;5;153musername[39m you already
    have. Buys you the tracking you asked for at near-zero cost; costs nothing; rules out tracking a stable ID across
    username changes. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mCaller supplies a known ID[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m where the submit handler must obtain a real user ID
    from somewhere that does not exist yet (a new form field, a session, or a lookup call). Buys a real identity; costs
    building that source; this one is [3mnot[0m cheap to change later, because whatever supplies the ID becomes an interface.
    This would also stop being bounded — it names structure the repo does not have — and I'd re-classify before touching
    code.

  [1mMy recommendation: the client-generated tracking ID.[0m It is the only reading that both matches your words ("add a
  [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter") and is actually implementable against this repo today — the third option can't be built without
  inventing an ID source first, and the second doesn't give you the parameter you asked for.
